= Consciousness (technical) =

== Operational distinctions ==
'''Phenomenal consciousness''' concerns subjective experience; '''access consciousness''' concerns availability of information to cognitive operations, report and flexible control. Wakefulness, vigilance, metacognitive access and verbal output are separate measurement dimensions. Unconscious information processing and task performance make it essential to distinguish markers from explanatory models.

== Competing computational and neurobiological hypotheses ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Framework !! Proposed explanatory focus !! Limitation
|-
| Global neuronal workspace theory || Global availability or broadcast of selected content among specialized processors || Neural ignition and access predictions are not identical to direct tests of experience
|-
| Integrated information theory || System's intrinsic causal organization and integrated information || Formal measure and interpretation are contentious; Shannon bits alone are insufficient
|-
| Higher-order approaches || A representation of a state as one's own mental state || Higher-order monitoring need not straightforwardly establish phenomenology
|-
| Recurrent processing approaches || Recurrent sensory/neural dynamics || Recurrence is common in computation and may not be sufficient alone
|}

A preregistered empirical collaboration tested specified predictions of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theories; measured neural dynamics did not cleanly support all critical predictions of either. This narrows candidate models but does not prove a universal negative or positive diagnosis of consciousness.

== Algorithmic similarity versus phenomenal attribution ==
An agent implemented on silicon can have perception, planning, self-modeling, memory and continuity across restarts. Those functions are measurable in engineering terms. Deriving subjective experience from them would require a defended bridge principle and suitable evidence, which is not an established universal theorem. Conversely, physical substrate alone is not an adequate scientific test without a theory.

== Evidence and provenance ==
Empirical markers include behavior, neural recording, no-report paradigms, perturbational measurements and clinical tests. Each operationalizes a different aspect and carries confounds. Cross-species and artificial-system inference requires attention to homologous mechanisms, behavioral alternatives and anthropomorphism.

